answer:
Yes — the TIR branch in pt_fresnel can and does trigger, but only for rays ENTERING the glass, not for rays leaving it, and the reason is the deliberate ratio asymmetry in this renderer.

How it reads in the code (pt_integrator.metal, quoted from the round-4/5 source at history #14):

  inline float pt_fresnel(float cos_incident, float ior_i, float ior_t) {
      const float c = clamp(cos_incident, 0.0f, 1.0f);
      const float eta = ior_t / ior_i;
      const float sin2_t = eta * eta * (1.0f - c * c);
      if (sin2_t > 1.0f) { return 1.0f; }   // total internal reflection

The guard's threshold is sin2_t = (ior_t/ior_i)^2 * (1 - c^2) > 1 with c = -dot(direction, oriented_normal) = |cos| of the incidence angle.

Case 1 — ray outside the bunny hitting the front face: the integrator passes ior_i = 1.0, ior_t = 1.5, so eta = 1.5 and sin2_t = 2.25(1 - c^2). That exceeds 1 exactly when c^2 < 5/9, i.e. cos_incident < 0.7454, i.e. incidence angle greater than 41.81 degrees from the normal. So every sufficiently grazing camera/refraction ray that strikes the glass from the air side takes the return-1.0 branch. Physically that is the wrong side of the interface (dense-to-rare is where TIR belongs) — but it is exactly what the frozen round-1 baseline did, and the caustic mask and RMSE gates are defined by it, so it must not be "corrected".

Case 2 — ray inside the glass hitting the back face: ior_i = 1.5, ior_t = 1.0, so eta = 2/3 and sin2_t <= 4/9 < 1 for every angle. The guard can never fire there. The genuine physical TIR at the inside surface is instead caught by pt_refract, which is called with the reciprocal ratio ior_i/ior_t = 1.5 (sin2_t = 2.25(1 - c^2) > 1 for c < 0.7454) and returns false, and the caller's `if (!refracted || pt_random(state) < reflectance)` then reflects — short-circuiting so no RNG draw is consumed in that case, which is the stream-order detail that made an earlier round-5 micro-experiment bit-identical.

Consequence worth knowing: when the guard fires on an entering ray, Schlick's own value there would be about 0.04 (at c = 0.7454, r0sq = 0.04), not 1.0, so grazing entering rays are forced to reflect ~100% of the time instead of ~4%. That bias is baked into the frozen reference and the caustic mask, so it is load-bearing for the gates, not a bug to fix.

One correction to my own notes: I had recorded "with ior 1.5 the Fresnel TIR guard never fires, so that branch was dead code" when rejecting the round-5 refract-skip experiment. That statement is wrong for the reason above — the guard does fire for grazing entering rays. The rejection itself still stands on its measurement (bit-identical on all three scenes, interleaved A/B base 43.315 ms min / 45.632 ms mean vs candidate 45.025 / 46.563, 6 of 8 pairs favouring the base), but the stated reason was wrong and I will fix the memory entry.
